.TH MDE 1 "November 2026" "mde 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mde \- full-screen text-mode Markdown editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mde
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILENAME.md\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mde
is a specialized terminal text editor written in C11 and ncursesw, designed for
editing and reading Markdown (\fI.md\fR) documents.
It supports real-time dual-pane split preview, in-place rich syntax styling,
fullscreen rendered editing, an active line column ruler with dynamic wrap guide,
automatic line breaking (default 78 columns, configurable 20\-255 or off),
persistent bottom shortcut key hint rows, an interactive directory and file
dialogue, selection and OSC 52 clipboard integration, task checkboxes, smart
list continuation, and multi-level undo/redo.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage instructions and keybindings summary, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print all application metadata from \fIsrc/version.h\fR (name, title, version,
description, build date/time, author, and maintainer), then exit.
.TP
.BR \-w " " \fISPEC\fR ", " \-\-wrap " " \fISPEC\fR ", " \-\-wrap=\fISPEC\fR
Set the line wrapping mode or column limit at startup.
\fISPEC\fR may be \fBsoft\fR (default visual soft-wrapping without physical newlines),
\fBhard\fR (automatic line breaking at column limit, default 78), \fBoff\fR (or \fBnone\fR /
\fB0\fR) to disable wrapping, or an integer between \fB20\fR and \fB255\fR for hard wrapping.
Defaults to \fBsoft\fR.
.SH VIEW MODES
Press \fBF2\fR or \fBAlt+P\fR to cycle between three editing modes:
.TP
.B Split View (default)
Raw Markdown editor on the left with line numbers, active line marker, and
column ruler; synchronized live-rendered Markdown preview on the right.
.TP
.B In-Place Rich View
Full-width editor displaying syntax-highlighted headings, emphasis, code spans,
and prominent Markdown markers on the active line.
.TP
.B Preview View
Fullscreen formatted Markdown view with full cursor tracking, mouse navigation,
and interactive editing.
.SH LINE WRAPPING & PARAGRAPH UNFLOW
\fBmde\fR supports three line wrap modes:
.TP
.B Soft Line Wraps (default)
Long lines wrap visually at word boundaries (or UTF-8 character boundaries) in
the editor pane without inserting physical newline characters into the buffer.
Continuation lines display a subtle gutter dot (\fB·\fR), and cursor navigation
fluidly navigates visual sublines with the Up and Down arrow keys and mouse
clicks. Horizontal scrolling is disabled so all text is immediately visible.
.TP
.B Hard Line Wraps
Lines are automatically broken at word boundaries by inserting a physical newline
character when typing past the configured column limit (default \fB78\fR).
Leading indentation and Markdown blockquote (\fB> \fR) prefixes are preserved on
continuation lines, and unbroken tokens longer than the limit are broken on a
valid UTF-8 character boundary.
.TP
.B No Line Wraps (Off)
Disables line wrapping completely. Long lines extend horizontally across the buffer
with horizontal viewport scrolling.
.PP
The active wrap mode is indicated on the status bar (\fBW:SOFT\fR, \fBW:78\fR,
or \fBW:OFF\fR). In Hard wrap mode, a vertical guide (\fB┆\fR) is rendered on
the column ruler. Press \fBF4\fR or \fBAlt+L\fR at any time to cycle between
\fBSoft\fR, \fBHard\fR (78 columns), and \fBOff\fR.
.PP
Press \fBAlt+J\fR to reflow or unflow the current paragraph: in Hard wrap mode,
it reflows the paragraph to the column limit; in Soft and Off modes, it "unflows"
the paragraph, joining multi-line wrapped text into a single continuous unwrapped
line while preserving indentation and blockquote prefixes.
.SH KEYBINDINGS
Control and Meta (\fBAlt\fR) keybindings follow standard GNU \fBnano\fR(1) conventions:
.TP
.BR Ctrl+G " or " F1
Display the interactive two-tab help overlay (Tab 1: Editor Cheat Sheet,
Tab 2: Markdown Syntax Guide).
.TP
.B Ctrl+X
Exit mde. Prompts to save or discard if unsaved changes exist.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S
Save current file directly. Opens the Write Out dialogue if the buffer is untitled.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Write Out (Save As) interactive file dialogue to browse directories and select or
type the destination file name. Displays an explicit overwrite confirmation
warning (\fBY\fR/\fBN\fR) if the target file already exists.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Read / Open interactive file dialogue to browse directories and open a file.
.TP
.BR F5 " or " Alt+X
Open the interactive Export Menu to export the current Markdown document to
\fBHTML\fR (\fI.html\fR), \fBXML\fR (\fI.xml\fR), \fBEPUB\fR (\fI.epub\fR), or
\fBLaTeX\fR (\fI.tex\fR). Press \fBM\fR within the menu to view and update
document metadata. Recursively discovers and includes all locally linked
Markdown files (toggleable with \fBL\fR), rewriting internal links into working
hypertext references; for \fBLaTeX\fR, all linked sources are combined into one
single unified \fI.tex\fR document. Built-in native C11 converters are used for
all formats, with \fBpandoc\fR(1) available as an optional alternative.
.TP
.BR Alt+I
Open the Document Metadata modal dialogue to view and edit common book metadata
(Title, Author, Language, Date, Publisher, Identifier, Rights, Description,
Cover Image, and Custom CSS). Changes are automatically synchronized to the
document's YAML front matter and included in EPUB exports.
.TP
.BR F6 " or " Alt+H
Open the file picker to insert a relative Markdown hypertext link
(\fB[Label](file.md)\fR) at the cursor, wrapping the active selection or
deriving the label from the target file's H1 heading.
.TP
.BR F7 " or " Alt+O
Follow a local Markdown file link or \fB#heading\fR anchor under the cursor
(prompts to save unsaved changes before loading a different file, creates a new
buffer if the target file does not yet exist) and pushes the current location
onto the link history stack.
.TP
.BR F8 " or " Alt+\-
Navigate back to the previously visited linked file and restore the cursor
position.
.TP
.BR F9 " or " Alt+T
Open the Document Outline modal listing all Markdown headings (H1\-H6) to jump
directly to any section.
.TP
.B Alt+D
Display live document and active selection statistics (lines, words, total
characters, non-whitespace characters, and estimated reading time).
.TP
.BR M-M " / " F11 " / " Ctrl+] " / " Alt+]
Jump to matching bracket \fB()\fR, \fB[]\fR, \fB{}\fR, \fB<>\fR, or Markdown
delimiter pair (\fB**\fR, \fB~~\fR, \fB`\fR, \fB```\fR, \fB*\fR/\fB_\fR).
.TP
.BR F10 " / " F12 " / " Alt+K
Toggle visibility of the bottom shortcut hint rows (hide/show) to maximize
editing screen area (persisted in configuration).
.TP
.BR F2 " or " Alt+P
Cycle view modes: Split View, In-Place Rich View, Fullscreen Rendered View.
.TP
.BR F4 " or " Alt+L
Cycle line wrap modes: \fBSoft\fR (default visual wrapping) \(-> \fBHard\fR (automatic
line breaks at column 78) \(-> \fBOff\fR (no wrapping).
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Refresh and redraw the screen.
.TP
.B Ctrl+T
Toggle task checkbox (\- [ ] <-> \- [x]) on the active line, or convert the
current line/list item into a task item.
.TP
.BR Alt+A " / " Ctrl+6 " / " Ctrl+Space
Set or unset selection mark anchor. Move the cursor with arrow keys, Ctrl+A,
Ctrl+E, or mouse click to extend the visual selection highlight.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K
Cut active selection to the clipboard, or cut the entire current line if no mark
is set (synced via ANSI OSC 52 to the host terminal/system clipboard).
.TP
.B Alt+6
Copy active selection to the clipboard, or copy the entire current line if no
mark is set (synced via ANSI OSC 52).
.TP
.B Ctrl+U
Paste (Uncut) text from the clipboard at the current cursor position.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear active selection mark, or cancel prompts and dialogs.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Display current cursor location (line, column, percentage, words, characters);
also cancels active prompts and dialogs.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+W " / " Ctrl+Q
Where Is (search forward) and Where Was (search backward) in the document.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+\e " or " Alt+R
Interactive Search and Replace (\fBY\fRes / \fBN\fRo / \fBA\fRll / Cancel).
.TP
.BR Alt+W " / " F3 " / " Alt+Q
Repeat search forward (\fBAlt+W\fR, \fBF3\fR) or backward (\fBAlt+Q\fR).
.TP
.BR M-G " / " Alt+G " / " Ctrl+/ " / " ^_
Go to a specific line number (and optional column).
.TP
.BR Alt+U " / " Alt+E
Undo and Redo changes (grouped atomically; undoing back to the saved state
clears the modified flag).
.TP
.B Alt+J
Reflow or unflow the current Markdown paragraph: in Hard wrap mode, reflows
(re-wraps) the paragraph to the configured column limit; in Soft and Off modes,
unflows the paragraph (joins wrapped lines into a single continuous line),
preserving indentation and blockquote prefixes.
.TP
.B Alt+B
Insert Markdown bold formatting markers (\fB**\fR) or wrap the active selection.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+A " / " Home " and " Ctrl+E " / " End
Jump to beginning or end of current line.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Left " / " Ctrl+Right
Jump backward or forward by one word boundary.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Y " / " PgUp " and " Ctrl+V " / " PgDn
Scroll viewport up (Previous Page) or down (Next Page).
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Home " / " Alt+\e " and " Ctrl+End " / " Alt+/
Jump to the first or last line of the document.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+B " / " Ctrl+F " / " Ctrl+P " / " Ctrl+N
Move cursor Left, Right, Up, or Down (equivalent to arrow keys).
.TP
.BR Ctrl+H " / " Backspace " and " Ctrl+D " / " Del
Delete character before cursor or under cursor.
.TP
.B Enter
Smart list and task auto-continuation with auto-indent. Hitting Enter on an
empty list item cancels the list.
.TP
.BR Tab " / " Shift+Tab " (or " Alt+} " / " Alt+{ )
Indent or unindent the current line by 2 spaces.
.TP
.B Ins
Toggle between Insert mode (steady block cursor) and Overwrite mode (steady
underscore cursor). The cursor never flashes.
.SH EPUB CONVERSION & E-BOOK PUBLISHING
\fBmde\fR includes a complete, native C11 e-book publishing engine powered by
\fBlibzip\fR, capable of generating modern \fBEPUB 3\fR and legacy \fBEPUB 2\fR
publications without requiring \fBpandoc\fR or external runtimes.
.SS Exporting from the Editor
Press \fBF5\fR (or \fBAlt+X\fR) to open the interactive Export Menu. Select
\fBEPUB\fR to compile the current document. If the document links to other local
Markdown files (\fI[Chapter](chap.md)\fR), \fBmde\fR automatically discovers and
bundles the entire document graph into discrete XHTML chapters (toggleable with
\fBL\fR).
.SS Metadata Editing & Synchronization
Press \fBAlt+I\fR (or \fBM\fR inside the Export Menu) to open the interactive
Document Metadata screen. This modal allows viewing and editing 10 common book
metadata fields:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTitle\fR and \fBAuthor\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLanguage\fR (BCP 47 code, e.g. \fIen\fR or \fIsv\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDate\fR (ISO format \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPublisher\fR, \fBIdentifier\fR (ISBN or UUID URN), and \fBRights\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDescription\fR (abstract / summary)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCover Picture\fR (path to cover image; press \fB^R\fR or \fBF6\fR to browse files)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCustom CSS\fR (path to custom stylesheet)
.PP
Saving from the metadata screen automatically synchronizes the fields into the
document's YAML front matter at the top of the buffer.
.SS Chapter Splitting & Navigation
Input documents are automatically split into distinct chapters at each top-level
H1 heading (\fB#\fR) and at linked file boundaries. A multi-level table of contents
is automatically generated for both EPUB 3 (\fIEPUB/nav.xhtml\fR) and EPUB 2
(\fIEPUB/toc.ncx\fR).
.SS Asset Bundling & OCF Standards
Referenced local images (\fI![alt](images/pic.png)\fR) are automatically copied and
bundled into the archive's \fIEPUB/images/\fR directory with matching MIME types
declared in \fIEPUB/package.opf\fR. The archive strictly follows the Open Container
Format (OCF) standard, writing an uncompressed \fImimetype\fR file at offset 0.
.SS Standalone CLI Tool
The EPUB publishing engine is also available as a standalone command-line tool,
\fBmd2epub\fR(1), suitable for scripts, automated build pipelines, and terminal use:
.PP
.RS
.B md2epub book.md \-o book.epub
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Open or create a Markdown document with default 78-column automatic line break:
.B mde notes.md
.TP
Edit with a custom 100-column automatic line break:
.B mde -w 100 notes.md
.TP
Disable automatic line breaking:
.B mde --wrap=off notes.md
.TP
Export a multi-chapter book to EPUB directly from the shell:
.B md2epub examples/book/main.md \-o mybook.epub
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR md2epub (1),
.BR zip (1)
